    <item>
      <title>F5 scrambles to fix more critical BIG-IP security flaws</title>
      <link>https://www.csoonline.com/article/4251711/f5-scrambles-to-fix-more-critical-big-ip-security-flaws-2.html</link>
      <description>&lt;div id="remove_no_follow"&gt;
		&lt;div class="grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column "&gt;
					  &lt;div class="col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg"&gt;
						&lt;div class="article-column__content"&gt;
&lt;section class="wp-block-bigbite-multi-title"&gt;&lt;div class="container"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/section&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;F5 has released a batch of out of band patches for &lt;a href="https://www.networkworld.com/article/4143922/f5-brings-new-visibility-and-ai-controls-to-big-ip-nginx.html"&gt;BIG-IP Access&lt;/a&gt; Policy Manager (APM) and BIG-IP iRule programmable scripting, five of which are rated 9.8 (Critical) in severity. BIG-IP Access Policy Manager (APM) is the vendor’s application access management and security product while BIG-IP iRules lets network administrators customize how BIG-IP handles application traffic.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;All five of the patches are stack-based buffer overflows that could let an unauthenticated threat actor terminate the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) and execute code remotely on vulnerable systems.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;F5 described them as follows:&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;ul class="wp-block-list"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163946" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-121805&lt;/a&gt;: When an iRule that processes client traffic using the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;PEM:subscriber&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; command is configured on a virtual server, undisclosed requests can cause the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to terminate and may lead to remote code execution (RCE);&lt;/li&gt;



&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163940" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-120292&lt;/a&gt;: When an HTTP profile and an iRule using the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;MR:message route&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; command are configured on a virtual server, undisclosed requests can cause the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to terminate and may lead to remote code execution (RCE);&lt;/li&gt;



&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163939" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-120285&lt;/a&gt;: When an HTTP profile and an iRule containing the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;PSC:ip_address&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; command are configured on a virtual server, undisclosed requests can cause the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to terminate and may lead to remote code execution (RCE);&lt;/li&gt;



&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163941" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-116953&lt;/a&gt;: When an iRule that processes client traffic using the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;GENERICMESSAGE:route&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; command is configured on a virtual server, undisclosed requests can cause the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to terminate and may lead to remote code execution (RCE);&lt;/li&gt;



&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163937" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-123637&lt;/a&gt;: When BIG-IP APM is provisioned and a per-request policy that includes the Client Information agent and Endpoint Security agents is applied to a virtual server, undisclosed requests can cause the Traffic Management Microkernel (TMM) to terminate and may lead to remote code execution (RCE).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The company said there are no mitigations for the flaws and advised customers to install the hotfixes on vulnerable devices, and to upgrade vulnerable versions without a listed fix to a patched version.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;In the same batch of out of band patches, F5 also addressed &lt;a href="https://my.f5.com/manage/s/article/K000163942" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank"&gt;CVE-2026-126822&lt;/a&gt;, a high severity integer underflow vulnerability in BIG-IP APM SAML that could also crash TMM and lead to denial of service.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;This is the second &lt;a href="https://www.networkworld.com/article/4225726/f5-fixes-actively-exploited-zero-day-flaw-in-big-ip-apm-2.html"&gt;critical issue&lt;/a&gt; in two months for APM; September’s zero-day bug was already being exploited when it was patched. F5 did not indicate that any of the current batch is as yet being exploited but given the &lt;a href="https://www.csoonline.com/article/4074945/network-security-devices-endanger-orgs-with-90s-era-flaws.html"&gt;growing trend of attacks on edge devices&lt;/a&gt; by criminals and nation-state threat actors, admins should update systems as soon as possible.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article originally appeared on &lt;a href="https://www.networkworld.com/article/4251676/f5-scrambles-to-fix-more-critical-big-ip-security-flaws.html"&gt;NetworkWorld&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.csoonline.com/article/4251711/f5-scrambles-to-fix-more-critical-big-ip-security-flaws-2.html</guid>
