    <item>
      <title>Clever AI prompt ideas every security leader should steal</title>
      <link>https://www.csoonline.com/article/4248701/clever-ai-prompt-ideas-every-security-leader-should-steal.html</link>
      <description>&lt;div id="remove_no_follow"&gt;
		&lt;div class="grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column "&gt;
					  &lt;div class="col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg"&gt;
						&lt;div class="article-column__content"&gt;
&lt;section class="wp-block-bigbite-multi-title"&gt;&lt;div class="container"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/section&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;For all the talk of AI use by attackers and defenders, little attention is paid to ways CISOs and their security pros can leverage AI uniquely to expediate tasks, cut through the noise, facilitate decision-making, and potentially save big on auditing.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Several specific AI prompts can help security leaders and staff handle multiple critical tasks not typically associated with AI for cyber defense, such as risk management, compliance, policy development, and critical thinking.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Developing truly useful prompts isn’t particularly easy, however, because it often requires a significant amount of research and effort, not to mention lots of fine-tuning, which can take security teams away from other critical tasks. Fortunately, the security community is here to help, with advice on unique prompt ideas that have greatly aided them in strengthening security, facilitating decision-making, and saving time for hands-on defense.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="create-an-adversarial-assistant-to-control-damage-and-cut-down-ai-slop"&gt;Create an adversarial assistant to control damage — and cut down AI slop&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Dzmitry Romanovadvocates establishing an AI prompt that acts as a second security reviewer to help catch latent problems before any damage occurs. The key, though, is to give it an adversarial twist so that it looks at a situation from the attacker’s perspective while critiquing the human security reviewer’s ideas.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“What am I missing? What attack vectors or edge cases should I inspect?” is the kind of questions Romanov, cybersecurity team lead and senior security engineer at the Belarusian State University of Informatics and Radioelectronics and cybersecurity team lead with Vention, an AI-enabled software development company, such a prompt can help with.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“This approach works most effectively when analyzing architecture or code, while reviewing penetration test results, and auditing cloud configurations, as well as when modeling threats and assessing risks,” Romanov says.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Philippe Dourassov, AI pentest lead at Aikido Security, offers another version of the adversarial agent prompt — in this case against agent helpers.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;When agents are used to generate security reports, engineers often find themselves buried under irrelevant findings — noise, says Dourassov. However, when a second agent is deployed to analyze the quality of another agent’s work, and correct its output, the result can be surprisingly efficient.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“The first agent has been given a task and will do everything it can to convince the humans that its results are relevant and critical, as this is what the agent would consider a good job,” Dourassov says, noting that his adversarial agent is basically a reviewer. “Its task is to eliminate noise, rectify claims, and highlight the true importance of the first agent’s result.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The agent’s definition of a good job, Dourassov says, is being able to eliminate noise and supply only signal to the human user. “Therefore, it’s essential to use an adversarial prompt to review agents’ output, so we ensure we don’t face security fatigue from AI slop.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class="wp-block-heading"&gt;Take care of business&lt;strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Brandyn Fisher, senior penetration testing technical lead at business and technology consulting firm Centric Consulting, offers three prompt ideas that CISOs can deploy on a regular basis to aid with their work.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The first Fisher uses is a vendor due diligence prompt. “I feed it our organization’s risk thresholds and the vendor in question, and it benchmarks that vendor, sweeps the Internet for security pages, breach history, customer reviews, complaints, and recent news, then returns a structured report I can review and validate quickly,” he explains.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Fisher also recommends crafting a professional writing prompt that takes technical analysis and reframes it into language a CIO or board will actually engage with.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Gen AI pioneer Kevin Surace offered a similar prompt idea to attendees of this year’s &lt;a href="https://event.foundryco.com/cso-conference-awards/awards/"&gt;CSO Conference and Awards&lt;/a&gt; in May. “Translate the SOC for the boardroom. This is important. They don’t really understand what’s going on,” Surace said. “The prompt here: ‘You are writing the cyber risk section of the board deck. You have four minutes of board time. Below is ninety days of security operations data. Produce three slides for a board that does not know what lateral movement means.’ This is so useful.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Fisher’s third prompt for CISOs is a tech debt reduction prompt “where I list our technology stack and have AI flag underutilized tools, overlapping capabilities, and consolidation opportunities,” he says.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Fisher advises building each of his recommendations as a standing agent, project, or skill, or whatever your AI platform supports, so you have a static, repeatable spot to run it instead of rewriting the prompt from scratch every time.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Don’t wait for a perfect first version, Fisher adds. “Get an initial prompt working, even if it’s rough, and have AI help draft the instructions themselves.” Expect to revise it several times before it’s reliable.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="challenge-your-policies-and-content"&gt;Challenge your policies and content&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;At the CSO Conference, Surace also highlighted how CISOs can use AI prompts to audit their policy libraries.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“You are a compliance auditor. I am giving you our policy library and the current text of NIST CSF 2.0, ISO 27001:2022 Annex A, SEC Form 8-K Item 1.05, and the EU AI Act. Produce three lists,” his example prompt began, directing the LLM to find contradictions, gaps, and stale language — the kind of analysis big firms do for six figures in 12 weeks, he noted.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Olivia Brännlund, CISO at Outpost24, a cybersecurity and risk management firm, regularly uses a similar prompt that turns AI into an internal auditor and security advisor. The tool reviews policy, processes, risk assessment, incident reports, contract clauses, and specific customer requirements.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Brännlund believes that organizations that treat AI as an expert reviewer typically obtain more value than organizations that treat it as a content generator. The key, she states, “isn’t using AI to write content, but using it to challenge content.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Tying together policy review and the adversarial assistant concept, Surace advised CSO conference attendees to also “red team your own runbook.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“You are the operations lead for Scattered Spider. I am giving you our incident response playbook, our detection coverage matrix, and our on-call tree. Identify the five places this runbook fails,” his prompt started, probing for wrong assumptions, bottlenecks, escalation path weaknesses, and so on.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“It’s totally worth doing this,” he said. “You will have more information in an hour than you, than you’ve ever had.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="vet-your-agents-capabilities-and-weaknesses"&gt;Vet your agent’s capabilities and weaknesses&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Chris Hughes advises asking your model what your AI agents can actually do.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“Give it an agent’s configuration, tool manifest, or MCP server definitions and ask it to summarize the agent’s effective permissions, the blast radius if it’s compromised, and whether a human approval step exists anywhere in the workflow,” says Hughes, who teaches in the CISO and Leading Enterprise Agentic AI Development Certificate Program at Carnegie Mellon University’s Heinz College. “Most organizations are deploying agents faster than they’re documenting what those agents can reach, and this closes the gap in minutes.”&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;The model turns work that normally requires a senior architect into something an analyst can run at design review, before deployment, and after every change. It also surfaces assumptions the team stopped questioning, Hughes says. The model doesn’t share your blind spots, so it tends to flag the over-permissioned tool or the missing approval gate that everyone walked past, he notes. Once tuned, the prompt becomes a control in its own right rather than a one-off exercise.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="embrace-deep-inspection"&gt;Embrace deep inspection&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Steve Tait, CTO at cloud security firm Skyhigh Security, suggests tailoring an AI prompt to take aim at AI prompts themselves, noting that perhaps the most important capability is a prompt response inspection that understands both data and intent.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“CISOs should be able to determine not only whether sensitive information is entering or leaving an AI service, but whether a user or agent is attempting something risky—such as prompt injection, jailbreaks, policy evasion, credential exposure or unauthorized data access,” he says, adding that as AI agents become more common, that same inspection and policy model needs to extend beyond human prompts to agent-driven interactions.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;Tait believes that CISOs should think of prompt security as a platform control that needs to work across every major access path to AI, including Secure Web Gateway (SWG), zero trust network access, browser-based access, and APIs.&lt;/p&gt;



&lt;p class="wp-block-paragraph"&gt;“The goal should not be to plug a single gap for one AI application, but to establish a consistent policy and inspection layer wherever users or agents interact with AI services,” he explains. “This also means choosing an approach that can support hybrid environments, including organizations that still rely heavily on on-premises SWG infrastructure.”&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
